INT. TRENCH DUGOUT - NIGHT

Timber props, a guttering candle, a field radio crackling on an ammo crate. Shells thump overhead and dust sifts down.

CAPTAIN OSTRANDER, 40s, sits against the wall with his helmet in his lap. He reaches for the handset, lifts it, and puts it down. He lifts it again.

OSTRANDER
Battalion, this is Fox Two. Do you read?

LT. PRUITT (V.O.)
(radio, faint)
Fox Two, Battalion. We read you. Say your status.

He closes his eyes. When he opens them he is steadier.

OSTRANDER
We are holding. Half the company is gone. He wants us to fall back.

PRUITT (V.O.)
(radio)
Negative, Fox Two. Hold position. Reinforcements at oh-four-hundred.

Ostrander laughs, short and dry. He wipes his face with a gloved hand. A shell lands closer. The candle gutters out.

OSTRANDER
They'll be late.

PRUITT (V.O.)
(radio)
Say again?

He sets the handset in its cradle, and in the dark, listens.
